1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,469
One fried chicken costs 23,000 won.

2
00:00:02,469 --> 00:00:05,573
How much of it does the owner actually
keep?

3
00:00:06,023 --> 00:00:08,415
Here's the answer first.

4
00:00:08,415 --> 00:00:10,507
Delivery: 24 percent.

5
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:12,401
Pickup: 49 percent.

6
00:00:12,401 --> 00:00:15,390
Same chicken, twice the share.

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:18,387
We'll follow three things.

8
00:00:18,387 --> 00:00:25,441
Where the 23,000 won goes, what changes
with pickup, and what we can do.

9
00:00:25,891 --> 00:00:29,271
Let's take one 23,000-won delivery
chicken.

10
00:00:29,271 --> 00:00:32,573
We'll split it using the reported figures.

11
00:00:33,023 --> 00:00:35,319
The biggest part is ingredients.

12
00:00:35,319 --> 00:00:38,979
Chicken, oil and other ingredients cost
11,756 won.

13
00:00:39,429 --> 00:00:41,412
Let's do the math.

14
00:00:41,412 --> 00:00:46,370
11,756 divided by 23,000 is about 51
percent.

15
00:00:46,370 --> 00:00:48,022
More than half.

16
00:00:48,472 --> 00:00:52,057
Next is the fee paid to the delivery
platform.

17
00:00:52,057 --> 00:00:53,225
It's 5,729 won.

18
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:58,240
5,729 divided by 23,000 is about 25
percent.

19
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:01,559
Sell four, and one goes to fees.

20
00:01:02,009 --> 00:01:05,064
After tax, the owner keeps 5,516 won.

21
00:01:05,064 --> 00:01:08,201
Less than half of the ingredient cost.

22
00:01:08,651 --> 00:01:13,360
5,516 divided by 23,000 is about 24
percent.

23
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:17,854
Split four ways, the owner gets one slice.

24
00:01:18,304 --> 00:01:24,337
In short: ingredients 51, fees 25, owner
24 percent.

25
00:01:24,337 --> 00:01:26,773
Together, almost 100.

26
00:01:27,223 --> 00:01:31,669
But does all 5,516 won go into the owner's
pocket?

27
00:01:31,669 --> 00:01:34,514
Some costs aren't taken out yet.

28
00:01:34,964 --> 00:01:36,166
Labor and rent.

29
00:01:36,166 --> 00:01:41,133
The owner's share in this data doesn't
subtract those two yet.

30
00:01:41,583 --> 00:01:43,180
Let's try an example.

31
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:45,994
Say the shop sells 50 chickens a day.

32
00:01:45,994 --> 00:01:48,048
This is just an assumption.

33
00:01:48,498 --> 00:01:51,781
5,516 times 50 is 275,800 won.

34
00:01:51,781 --> 00:01:56,267
That's from 1,150,000 won in daily sales.

35
00:01:56,717 --> 00:02:01,585
Then come staff wages, shop rent, and
electricity and gas.

36
00:02:01,585 --> 00:02:04,522
The real take-home is even smaller.

37
00:02:04,972 --> 00:02:08,112
So bigger sales don't mean bigger profit.

38
00:02:08,112 --> 00:02:10,486
You need to look at the ratios.

39
00:02:10,936 --> 00:02:14,314
Now let's order the same chicken for
pickup.

40
00:02:14,314 --> 00:02:18,613
If you collect it yourself, the platform
fee disappears.

41
00:02:19,063 --> 00:02:21,014
Let's add it up.

42
00:02:21,014 --> 00:02:27,478
5,516 plus the 5,729 fee is 11,245 won for
the owner.

43
00:02:27,928 --> 00:02:30,869
That's 49 percent, almost half.

44
00:02:30,869 --> 00:02:34,190
More than twice the delivery share.

45
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:37,769
11,245 divided by 5,516 is 2.04.

46
00:02:37,769 --> 00:02:42,268
One change in how you order doubles the
share.

47
00:02:42,718 --> 00:02:44,191
Back to our example.

48
00:02:44,191 --> 00:02:47,800
What if all 50 chickens a day were pickup
orders?

49
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:51,715
11,245 times 50 is 562,250 won.

50
00:02:51,715 --> 00:02:56,523
That's over 280,000 won more than
delivery.

51
00:02:56,973 --> 00:02:59,818
Of course, not every order can be pickup.

52
00:02:59,818 --> 00:03:02,732
On rainy or busy days, delivery is a must.

53
00:03:03,182 --> 00:03:06,254
So what do these numbers mean for me?

54
00:03:06,254 --> 00:03:08,330
Here are three takeaways.

55
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:11,720
First, if the shop is close, order pickup.

56
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:15,081
You pay the same, but the owner keeps much
more.

57
00:03:15,531 --> 00:03:20,718
Second, if it's your neighborhood
favorite, drop by in person.

58
00:03:20,718 --> 00:03:22,977
Shops love those customers.

59
00:03:23,427 --> 00:03:27,266
Third, look at the numbers behind the
price.

60
00:03:27,266 --> 00:03:31,630
Knowing how 23,000 won splits changes your
choice.

61
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,955
This doesn't mean delivery is bad.

62
00:03:34,955 --> 00:03:39,351
Convenience has a price, and it matters
who pays it.

63
00:03:39,801 --> 00:03:41,354
One last look.

64
00:03:41,354 --> 00:03:48,340
The owner keeps 5,516 won on delivery and
11,245 won on pickup.

65
00:03:48,790 --> 00:03:51,904
These figures come from published news
data.

66
00:03:51,904 --> 00:03:55,373
Actual amounts vary by shop, region and
platform.

67
00:03:55,823 --> 00:03:59,402
Are you a delivery person or a pickup
person?

68
00:03:59,402 --> 00:04:01,311
Tell us in the comments.

69
00:04:01,311 --> 00:04:03,459
We'll dig deeper next time.

70
00:04:03,909 --> 00:04:07,421
So, shall we look at that first number
again?

71
00:04:07,421 --> 00:04:11,478
Out of 23,000 won, the owner's share was
24 percent.

